{"body": "J's internal criticism includes: “The capacity limit already imposes one cross-booking constraint, so \"across-booking independence\" is not total; it is independence *given capacity*.” This sentence occurs in the paragraph introduced by “The criticism I would attach is internal to this construction”; J calls the within/across split a reading that “may not carve the disagreements cleanly” and says, “I record that objection rather than resolve it.” The target here is a tentative premise entertained in that unresolved internal criticism, not a claim that J has already adopted or executed a filtering policy.\n\nThe premise needs a distinction between a shared input to the availability report and a rule excluding joint reservations. C0 explicitly says availability can be negative. It gives capacity 10 and active holds summing to 12, producing -2, and treats over-reservation as allowed by the description. Sharing capacity in the subtraction does not by itself require discarding every combination whose sum exceeds capacity.\n\nIf the sentence means that only combinations at or below capacity are admissible, an ensuing account that uses that meaning adds an exclusion rule that changes the source's permitted reporting use. The capacity-10, total-12 case distinguishes that reading from an account that retains the records and reports -2. If instead the sentence means only that one capacity value participates in reporting the sum across bookings, this objection does not establish that J excludes anything; the account should explain that reading of “constraint” and whether its phrase “independence given capacity” still does the claimed work. No probability model is supplied, so the phrase does not here establish statistical conditional independence.\n\nThis criticism does not establish that any unnamed physical resource exists, or that per-booking alternatives necessarily compose freely. P9's physical-compatibility objection remains a conditional question about an additional constraint. An explicitly supplied physical-feasibility constraint could justify excluding a combination in that new account, but its role differs from the mere fact that reporting capacity is shared. Preserve P7's distinction between questioning rules and questioning records, the possibility of negative reported availability, and the difference between reporting the bag and claiming physical feasibility. No change to C0 or the frozen languages is requested. The issue is which uses the tentative capacity premise supports, and whether the construction's organization needs qualification as a result.\n", "commitments": "J's internal criticism includes: “The capacity limit already imposes one cross-booking constraint, so \"across-booking independence\" is not total; it is independence *given capacity*.” This sentence occurs in the paragraph introduced by “The criticism I would attach is internal to this construction”; J calls the within/across split a reading that “may not carve the disagreements cleanly” and says, “I record that objection rather than resolve it.” The target here is a tentative premise entertained in that unresolved internal criticism, not a claim that J has already adopted or executed a filtering policy.\n\nThe premise needs a distinction between a shared input to the availability report and a rule excluding joint reservations. C0 explicitly says availability can be negative. It gives capacity 10 and active holds summing to 12, producing -2, and treats over-reservation as allowed by the description. Sharing capacity in the subtraction does not by itself require discarding every combination whose sum exceeds capacity.\n\nIf the sentence means that only combinations at or below capacity are admissible, an ensuing account that uses that meaning adds an exclusion rule that changes the source's permitted reporting use. The capacity-10, total-12 case distinguishes that reading from an account that retains the records and reports -2. If instead the sentence means only that one capacity value participates in reporting the sum across bookings, this objection does not establish that J excludes anything; the account should explain that reading of “constraint” and whether its phrase “independence given capacity” still does the claimed work. No probability model is supplied, so the phrase does not here establish statistical conditional independence.\n\nThis criticism does not establish that any unnamed physical resource exists, or that per-booking alternatives necessarily compose freely. P9's physical-compatibility objection remains a conditional question about an additional constraint. An explicitly supplied physical-feasibility constraint could justify excluding a combination in that new account, but its role differs from the mere fact that reporting capacity is shared. Preserve P7's distinction between questioning rules and questioning records, the possibility of negative reported availability, and the difference between reporting the bag and claiming physical feasibility. No change to C0 or the frozen languages is requested. The issue is which uses the tentative capacity premise supports, and whether the construction's organization needs qualification as a result.\n"}